## 0.1 The one architecture change this audit forces: on-demand LLM loading

**The problem:** the original plan kept both a local LLM (~4 GB resident) and a Kokoro
TTS process (~1.5-2 GB resident, using the generic community image) always warm at the
same time. Added to the ~2.3 GB already used by existing containers, that's ~7.8-8.3 GB
minimum — before any Coolify build burst, before any of this box's other 15 containers
so much as blip. It does not fit with any real safety margin. (This is the same shape of
problem that got 8B rejected earlier for RAM reasons — a 4B LLM plus a second always-on
TTS process has the same footprint issue.)

**The fix:** the LLM does not need to be resident when nothing is happening — and for a
personal, async, cache-and-serve pipeline, nothing is happening most of the time. Switch
the local LLM runtime from raw `llama-server` to **Ollama**, which supports automatic
idle unload via `OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE` (e.g. `5m` — stays warm across a batch of
back-to-back articles, then fully unloads ~5 minutes after the last request). This is a
genuine architecture change from the earlier draft, made because the real numbers
demanded it, not a preference swap.

**Recomputed RAM budget:**
| State | Existing containers | narration-tts (Kokoro, always warm) | narration-orchestrator | narration-llm (Ollama) | Total | Free (of 7.8 GB) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Idle** (typical) | ~2.3 GB | ~1 GB | ~0.4 GB | 0 (unloaded) | **~3.7 GB** | **~4.1 GB** |
| **Actively generating** (brief burst) | ~2.3 GB | ~1 GB | ~0.4 GB | ~3.3 GB | **~7.0 GB** | **~0.8 GB** |

Idle state has real headroom for Coolify builds and normal fluctuation in the other 15
containers. The active-burst state is tight but transient (a minute or two per article,
not sustained) and non-blocking (nobody is waiting on it synchronously). Kokoro and the
LLM still never run concurrently within a job (§2's sequential design is unchanged) —
that's what keeps the active-burst number from being even higher.

**Model swapping stays just as easy, arguably easier:** upgrading later is
`ollama pull <new-model>` (or importing a new GGUF via a Modelfile) plus one env var
change — no manual GGUF file management required the way raw llama-server needs.

**Verify before relying on this:**
- Confirm Qwen3.5-4B is available directly in Ollama's model library, or import the
  already-verified GGUF (`unsloth/Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF`, `Qwen3.5-4B-Q4_K_M.gguf`, ~2.74 GB,
  Apache-2.0) via a Modelfile (`FROM ./Qwen3.5-4B-Q4_K_M.gguf`) if it isn't. Some Ollama
  versions support pulling a Hugging Face GGUF directly (`ollama pull
  hf.co/unsloth/Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF:Q4_K_M`) — check the installed version's support before
  assuming either path.
- **Do not assume this VPS can reach huggingface.co.** The audit explicitly did not test
  this. Build it as a self-healing check, not a one-time assumption: on container start,
  check a mounted volume path first for the GGUF; if present, use it and skip the network
  entirely; if absent, attempt the HF pull; if that also fails, halt cleanly with a clear
  Telegram alert naming the exact file/path needed, rather than crash-looping
  indefinitely. **Concrete fallback method: plain `scp`/`rsync` over SSH to place the
  2.74 GB GGUF onto the mounted volume** — no paid service, no extra tooling. If you
  already have SSH access to this VPS, attempt this automatically as part of the
  fallback; if not, this is a single one-time manual command for the user to run, after
  which the file lives permanently on the volume and this path never needs to trigger
  again.
- After first deploy, measure **actual** resident memory with `docker stats` (not the
  estimate above) before finalizing the memory limit — set the real cap from measured
  behavior, not the projection.

