## 0.3 Admission control (the concrete fix for the tight active-burst margin)

Rather than trading away context size or article length for more static headroom, make
the tight moment self-protecting: **check real conditions before launching a job, don't
just hope the design-time estimate holds.**

- **Single-flight enforcement:** the arq worker processes narration jobs with
  **concurrency = 1**, system-wide, always — an enforced setting, not an assumption based
  on single-user behavior. A burst of newly-ingested articles queues cleanly instead of
  ever running two jobs' RAM footprints at once.
- **Pre-launch RAM gate:** immediately before starting the LLM step of any job, check
  current free RAM (VPS stats endpoint or `/proc/meminfo`). If free RAM is below a safety
  floor (e.g. 1 GB — comfortably above the ~0.8 GB tightest-case margin from §0.1), defer
  the job with exponential backoff (30s, 60s, 120s...) rather than launching into a
  moment when, say, a Coolify build is also in flight. Only alert via Telegram if
  deferred more than twice in a row — routine single defers shouldn't be noisy.
- **Circuit breaker:** if `narration-llm` or `narration-tts` fail 3 times in a row across
  *different* articles (not retries of the same one), stop attempting server-side
  generation for a cooldown period (e.g. 15 minutes) and route those articles straight to
  the on-device fallback (§1.14) instead of continuing to hammer an unhealthy service.
  One Telegram alert when the breaker trips, one when it resets — not one per suppressed
  attempt underneath.

This turns "is 0.8 GB enough" from a static bet into a monitored, self-throttling system
that degrades gracefully (defer, then fall back) rather than risking an OOM.

