## 3. Deletion & reaper subsystem — build this like it must never fail silently

Unchanged from the earlier design: retry-and-verify (5 attempts, fast exponential
backoff with jitter), race-safe via a short-TTL Redis lock per cache_key, idempotent
("already gone" counts as success), and a mandatory hourly reaper with a 48h TTL that
independently guarantees cleanup regardless of whether the client's completion webhook
ever arrives. Deletes cover: primary + HD audio files, any temp/partial artifacts, the
article/script text, and the Redis cache entry (now specifically the `narration:`-prefixed
key in DB 1 — do not touch any other keys in the shared Redis instance). On exhausted
retries: Telegram alert (🚨), persistent failure log, retried again on the next reaper
pass — never a silent drop.

---

## 4. Telegram logging

Unchanged event set from the earlier design (synthesis success/failure, deletion
success/failure/retry-exhaustion, reaper summaries, unhandled exceptions), with one
addition given the tighter RAM budget:
- **⚠️ RAM pressure alert:** if `narration-llm`'s measured resident memory during an
  active job exceeds a defined threshold (e.g. 90% of its cap), or if the box's overall
  free RAM drops below a safety floor (e.g. 500 MB) during a job, alert immediately. This
  budget is tighter than the original design assumed, so visibility into real usage
  matters more here than it would on a roomier box.

Alongside Telegram (for human-visible alerts), have the orchestrator write structured
JSON logs for every pipeline event too. Telegram is for "notice this now" — structured
logs are for "debug this later." Don't rely on Telegram's message history as the only
record of what happened.

