## 5. Coolify deployment (corrected for `4.0.0-beta.468`)

- Auth: `Authorization: Bearer <id>|<secret>`, base URL `https://<host>/api/v1`. Confirm
  token has `write` + `deploy` abilities.
- **This Coolify version uses GET-style deploy triggers** (`GET /api/v1/deploy?uuid=...`)
  — do not apply a "POST-only" assumption meant for 4.2+.
- Create `narration-llm` (Ollama): `POST /api/v1/applications/dockerimage` with
  `docker_registry_image_name: "ollama/ollama"`, a pinned tag, an internal port (Ollama's
  default 11434 is fine — it doesn't collide with anything found in the audit), env vars
  `OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=5m`, `OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0`. Ollama has no dedicated `/health` route —
  configure Coolify's health/readiness check against `GET /api/tags` (lists loaded
  models) instead. **The creation call will not accept
  `limits_memory` on this Coolify version** — immediately follow up with an update call
  to apply a memory cap (start conservative, e.g. 4-4.5 GB, then tune from measured
  `docker stats` output) before any real load hits it.
- Create `narration-tts` (Kokoro, built from the in-repo int8 ONNX code — write a
  Dockerfile for it, since no external image applies here): internal port per the
  existing code's default (check the repo — see §0.2), memory cap ~1.2 GB.
- Create `narration-orchestrator` as a Coolify **Service** (`POST /api/v1/services`,
  `docker_compose_raw` **base64-encoded** — plain YAML 422s), containing the FastAPI app
  + arq worker, pointed at the existing shared Redis (DB 1, `narration:` prefix) rather
  than defining a new Redis in the compose file. Memory cap ~0.75 GB.
- None of these three services should publish a host port — internal Docker network only,
  reached by service name (e.g. `http://narration-llm:11434`, `http://narration-tts:<port>`).
- Stay under Coolify's 200 req/min API limit; batch env var writes where possible.
- After each deployment, run a real smoke test (a genuine test article through the full
  pipeline, confirm audio comes back and plays) before considering it done — not just a
  "container is running" check.

