## 6. Flutter: background playback, speed control, auto-advance, completed icon, fallback

- Stack: `audio_service` + `just_audio` + `just_audio_background` — genuinely new
  integration; `just_audio` is currently an unused dependency, `audio_service` doesn't
  exist yet.
- **Add** a new `mediaPlayback`-type foreground service for this feature. **Do not
  touch, repurpose, or interfere with the existing `dataSync` FGS** (`flutter_foreground_task`) —
  they coexist independently.
- Playback speed: `just_audio.setSpeed()`, 0.5x-2.0x, pitch-corrected (verify no
  chipmunk/slow-drawl artifacts at the extremes on a real device), persisted as a user
  preference.
- Auto-advance: dynamic `AudioHandler` queue fed from the backend, not a static
  playlist. Playback completion fires both the next-track advance and the completion
  webhook from §3 — same event, two effects.
- **Loading state for on-demand generation:** when opening an article triggers a
  cache-miss (§2.1's fallback path — pre-generation didn't finish in time, or a backlog
  item), show a clear, pleasant "preparing your audio…" indicator rather than a frozen
  or blank UI. This is the one path where generation latency is actually visible to the
  user, so make it feel intentional rather than broken.
- **Fallback:** if the orchestrator reports a failed/timed-out job for an article (or is
  simply unreachable), the Flutter client falls back to invoking the existing
  `ArticleTtsService`/`NativeTtsEngine` on-device path for that article instead of
  showing no audio option. Surface this distinction subtly in the UI (e.g. a small
  "on-device voice" indicator) so it's not confused with the narrated version.
- "Completed" icon: filled checkmark badge over the play button at the 95% threshold,
  title dimmed. **Recommended default** for the open question about post-completion
  behavior: keep the article visible with the checkmark for a while (e.g. until it ages
  out of the normal feed) rather than vanishing immediately when the underlying audio
  file gets deleted — abrupt disappearance right as something finishes feels jarring.
  This is a product-taste call, not a technical requirement — override it if you'd
  rather it disappear immediately.

